# Local AppArmor override for the `unprivileged_userns` profile.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#
# Ubuntu 24.04 (the GitHub-hosted `ubuntu-latest` runner image) enables
# restricted unprivileged user namespaces by default. A process that
# creates a user namespace transitions into AppArmor's
# `unprivileged_userns` profile, which grants `userns`, `network`,
# `file rwlkm /{,**}`, `signal`, `dbus`, `unix`, `mqueue` and `ptrace` --
# but NOT `mount`.
#
# The sandbox launcher's containment sequence needs `mount` inside that
# namespace: it makes mount propagation private, remounts the root
# filesystem read-only, and bind-mounts the artifact path writable.
# Without it the launcher cannot establish the boundary, and it
# correctly fails closed.
#
# MEASURED ON THE RUNNER
#
# Every mount variant was refused with EACCES, including a brand-new
# tmpfs with no inheritance involved:
#
#   mount(/, MS_REC|MS_PRIVATE)            EACCES
#   mount(/, MS_REC|MS_SLAVE)              EACCES
#   mount(/, MS_BIND)                      EACCES
#   mount(tmpfs, /tmp/newroot)             EACCES
#   mount(/, MS_REMOUNT|MS_BIND|MS_RDONLY) EACCES
#
# The same strategies all succeed on a host without the restriction, and
# the effective capability mask inside the new user namespace is fully
# populated in both cases.
#
# IMPORTANT: the later measurements showed that the LSM is NOT the
# denyer -- the profile after the unshare is still `docker-default
# (enforce)`, and the AppArmor audit log contains no DENIED record.
# The denial is the kernel's rule that a mount INHERITED into a
# user-namespace-owned mount namespace is locked, so its propagation
# cannot be changed.
#
# This override is therefore NOT the fix for that denial. It is kept
# because the namespace transition into `unprivileged_userns` is the
# documented Ubuntu 24.04 behaviour, and the launcher must be permitted
# to create a user namespace at all. If a deployment does not restrict
# unprivileged user namespaces, this override is unnecessary.
#
# WHY ONE RULE
#
# The launcher execs the parser as a CHILD process, and the stock
# profile already grants children exec permission through
# `allow pix /** -> &unprivileged_userns`. An explicit `x` rule cannot be
# added here: the stock `allow file rwlkm /{,**}` covers the same tree,
# and AppArmor rejects the merged rule with "conflicting x modifiers",
# which makes the whole profile fail to load.
#
# SCOPE
#
# This is applied ONLY to the GitHub-hosted CI runner, in the dedicated
# `linux-network-isolation` job. It is not applied to the production
# deployment, whose host AppArmor policy is the operator's to configure.
#
# It is NOT `flags=(unconfined)`, it does not disable AppArmor, and it
# grants no capability. The container still runs as UID 1000 with no
# host CAP_SYS_ADMIN, and the shipped seccomp profile still governs
# which syscalls are permitted at all.
#
# The delta is asserted by scripts/dev/verify_apparmor_delta.py.

  mount,
